<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shen Kuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1031–1095</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shen Kuo was a Chinese scholar-official and polymath of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a>'s reform government. In his Dream Pool Essays (completed about 1088) he described the magnetic needle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> and magnetic declination, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>, fossils and land formation, and many problems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, mathematics and engineering, making the work one of the richest records of Song science and technology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shen-kuo.jpg" alt="Shen Kuo" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans A. Rosbach · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beijing_Ancient_Observatory_20090715-19_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
